    "blurb": "The show packs a powerful Eyes Of The World excursion highlighted by Jerry’s new “wolf” tone and a fiery Truckin’ → Drums → Other One Jam transition. Let It Grow makes its live debut, though it feels tentative at times. Overall the performance is high‑energy with standout Bird Song and Here Comes Sunshine.",
    "review": "On September 7 1973 at Nassau Coliseum the Dead were in top form, delivering a set that still feels fresh for listeners today. The first half builds steadily through classic staples before bursting into a soaring Bird Song and an electrifying Playing In The Band that sets up the second‑set fireworks. Here Comes Sunshine opens the evening with bright optimism, leading into the historic debut of Let It Grow—while the song wanders a bit, it adds a memorable moment to the night. The centerpiece is the seamless Truckin’ → Drums → Other One Jam → Eyes Of The World suite, where Jerry’s newly unveiled “wolf” guitar tone drives an expansive, jazzy jam that showcases Phil’s deep, driving bass and Keith’s shimmering organ textures. Bob Weir provides gritty rhythm work despite a few slip‑ups early on, and Bill Kreutzmann lays down a solid, earthy pulse throughout. The show closes with a jubilant Sugar Magnolia and a classic Around & Around encore, leaving the audience buzzing long after the lights went out.",
